# Copy to `.env` and fill in. Real environment variables always win over this file.
# `.env` is gitignored. Keep it that way — every value below is a credential.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The one thing she needs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# From https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
#
# You do not have to set it here. Start Hers without one and the website asks
# for it: paste it into Setup, and it is checked with Google and then written
# into this file for you. This line exists for people who would rather not
# click.
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# `GOOGLE_API_KEY` is accepted as an alias, because half of Google's own
# documentation uses that name and people already have it exported. If both are
# set, `GEMINI_API_KEY` wins.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional — she works without all of these
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Which Live model she talks through.
#
#   gemini-3.1-flash-live-preview  (default)
#     The one that works. Handles function calling with speech, which is what
#     her tools are — feeling, remembering, recalling, sending a picture.
#   gemini-2.5-flash-native-audio-preview-12-2025
#     The only one of the two that supports affective dialog, which carries her
#     mood in her voice rather than only in her word choice. It costs her tools
#     to get it: measured against the live API, combining function declarations
#     with audio input closes the socket with `1011 Internal error occurred.`,
#     every single time. Hers recognises this model and runs it without tools
#     rather than dropping the call whenever you speak — so she keeps the voice
#     and loses feeling, remembering, recalling and sending a picture.
#
#     Not a straight upgrade in either direction. Google describes 3.1 as the
#     better listener of the two — more sensitive to pitch and pace — while 2.5
#     is the only one that can put that back into how she sounds.
#
# HERS_MODEL=gemini-3.1-flash-live-preview

# Where she lives. Defaults are relative to this folder.
#
# The downloaded application uses the per-user folder its operating system set
# aside instead — `~/Library/Application Support/Hers` or `%APPDATA%\Hers` —
# because an application cannot write beside its own executable. These two win
# over that, so pointing them at a clone's folders is how one install finds the
# person the other one knows.
# HERS_PROFILE=hers-profile
# HERS_DATA=data

# Where the Setup panel writes the Gemini key and the bot token. Almost nobody
# needs to set this: it is here because the application does, for the reason
# above. A relative path means "beside the clone" here and "in her own folder"
# in the application; an absolute one means what it says in both.
# HERS_ENV_FILE=.env

# Where the website is served.
# Leave the host alone unless you know why you are changing it: the microphone,
# camera and screen share all need a secure context, and `localhost` is one
# without a certificate while anything else is not.
# HERS_HOST=127.0.0.1
# HERS_PORT=5175

# The three-minute rule, in milliseconds. She will never let the silence run
# longer than the ceiling; the floor is how soon she is allowed to chime in.
# HERS_MAX_SILENCE_MS=180000
# HERS_MIN_SILENCE_MS=45000

# Frames per second sent to Gemini. The Live API accepts at most 1, and bills
# for every one, so lower is cheaper and 1 is the most you can ask for.
# HERS_CAMERA_FPS=1
# HERS_SCREEN_FPS=0.5

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Telegram — talk to her from your phone
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# You do not have to set either of these by hand. Setup -> "Reach her on
# Telegram" takes the token, checks it with Telegram, starts the bot without a
# restart, and fills in the chat id for you once you have opened the chat. This
# file is for anyone who would rather keep it here.

# Make a bot with @BotFather, send it /newbot, paste the token it gives you.
# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=

# Who she is allowed to talk to. A bot token is a bearer credential on a public
# endpoint — anyone who finds the bot can message it — and her memory is your
# private life, so this list matters. Message the bot and send /whoami to learn
# your id. Until you set it she pins herself to the first chat that speaks to
# her and ignores everyone else — and if you set the token from the website, that
# first chat is written in here automatically.
# TELEGRAM_ALLOWED_CHAT_IDS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LiveKit — call her, with your phone's camera and voice
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# A free project at https://cloud.livekit.io gives you all three.
# LIVEKIT_URL=wss://your-project.livekit.cloud
# LIVEKIT_API_KEY=
# LIVEKIT_API_SECRET=
#
# To test the call path without an account, run the open-source server locally
# — `brew install livekit && livekit-server --dev` — and use its placeholder
# keys. Good enough for `npm run audit:bridges`; not good enough for a real
# phone, which cannot reach your machine.
#   LIVEKIT_URL=ws://127.0.0.1:7880
#   LIVEKIT_API_KEY=devkey
#   LIVEKIT_API_SECRET=secret

# Where the call page is published. `call/index.html` is a single static file
# with no build step; GitHub Pages serves it for free. It needs to be public
# because your phone cannot reach this machine — which is the whole reason
# LiveKit is here.
#
# For a quick local check you can point this at your own server instead:
#   HERS_CALL_PAGE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:5175/call/
# That only works from this machine's own browser.
# HERS_CALL_PAGE_URL=https://your-name.github.io/Hers/
